-- ── 1. How the Water Cycle Works ── 102 → 224 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Water is constantly moving through Earth''s systems in a never-ending process called the water cycle. Energy from the sun causes water in oceans, lakes, and rivers to evaporate, turning liquid water into water vapor that rises into the atmosphere. As the vapor rises, it cools and condenses into tiny droplets, forming clouds. When enough droplets combine, precipitation falls as rain or snow. This water then flows into rivers, soaks into the ground, or collects in lakes — and the cycle begins again. The water cycle not only replenishes freshwater supplies but also helps regulate temperature and drive weather patterns around the globe.

The cycle does not move all water at the same speed. A molecule that evaporates from the ocean usually spends only about nine days in the atmosphere before falling again as precipitation. Water that soaks deep underground, by contrast, may sit in an aquifer for thousands of years before it returns to the surface. Ice behaves much the same way: water frozen into a glacier can stay locked in place for centuries. Most of Earth''s water is held in oceans and in ice rather than in rivers and lakes, so only a small share of it is available as fresh liquid water at any given moment. The cycle never stops, but its pace varies enormously from one storage place to the next.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%systems in a never-ending process called the%'
    AND passage NOT LIKE '%speed. A molecule that evaporates%';

-- ── 2. The History of Sign Language ── 105 → 222 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Sign language is a complete, complex language expressed through hand movements, facial expressions, and body posture rather than spoken words. Although deaf communities around the world have always developed informal signing systems, the first formal sign language school was established in Paris in 1760 by Abbé de l''Épée. His school later influenced American Sign Language (ASL) when a French teacher named Laurent Clerc traveled to the United States in 1817 to help found the American School for the Deaf in Hartford, Connecticut. Today, linguists recognize that sign languages are fully independent languages with their own grammar and syntax, not simply coded versions of spoken language.

Because each signing community shapes its own language, sign languages differ from one another about as much as spoken languages do. British Sign Language and American Sign Language are not mutually intelligible, even though both are used in countries where English is spoken; a signer from London and a signer from Chicago often cannot follow each other. Sign languages also use grammatical tools that speech cannot easily copy. A signer can place a person or object at a particular spot in the space in front of the body and then point back to that spot later to refer to it again, and can carry several pieces of information at once through handshape, movement, and facial expression together.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%Sign language is a complete, complex language%'
    AND passage NOT LIKE '%Because each signing community shapes%';

-- ── 3. The Roots of Jazz ── 105 → 220 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Jazz is a uniquely American art form that grew out of the vibrant African American communities of New Orleans in the late 1800s and early 1900s. It blends elements of African rhythmic traditions, blues, gospel, and European harmonic structures into an improvisational style unlike anything before it. Early jazz musicians played in venues called "juke joints" and at community celebrations, often creating music on the spot rather than reading from written scores. Jazz spread rapidly up the Mississippi River to Chicago and then to New York, where it flourished during the Harlem Renaissance. Today, jazz is recognized worldwide as one of America''s greatest cultural contributions.

Jazz never settled into one fixed style. In the 1920s and 1930s, as more players moved north during the Great Migration, bands grew larger and swing filled dance halls with arranged parts written for whole sections of horns. In the 1940s, younger musicians such as Charlie Parker and Dizzy Gillespie pushed back toward small groups, faster tempos, and harmonies that were much harder to dance to — a style that came to be called bebop. Later players stretched the music further still, borrowing from Latin, rock, and classical traditions. Each of these shifts drew complaints from listeners who preferred the older sound, yet that willingness to change is part of what has kept jazz alive.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%Jazz is a uniquely American art form%'
    AND passage NOT LIKE '%more players moved north during%';

