-- ── 5. The Brain During Sleep ── 106 → 218 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Sleep is far more than simply resting. While the body is still, the brain remains remarkably active, performing critical maintenance work. During deep sleep, the brain consolidates memories — transferring information from short-term to long-term storage. It also clears out waste products that build up during waking hours, including proteins linked to neurological diseases. During a phase called REM sleep, the brain processes emotions and may generate dreams. Sleep deprivation disrupts all of these processes, impairing memory, judgment, and emotional regulation. Scientists now understand that adequate sleep — typically eight to ten hours for teenagers — is not optional but essential for brain and body health.

Getting that sleep is harder for teenagers than it sounds. During adolescence the body''s internal clock shifts: the brain starts releasing melatonin, the hormone that signals sleepiness, later in the evening than it did in childhood. A teenager still wide awake late at night is often not being stubborn but following biology. Bright light from screens can push that signal back even further. Because school still begins early, the shortfall builds up night after night across the week, and sleeping late on Saturday does not fully undo it. Some school districts have responded by moving their start times later, and researchers who study those districts report improvements in student attendance and alertness.'
