-- ── 6. The Olympic Games Through Time ── 107 → 232 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'The Olympic Games, now a global sporting celebration held every four years, began in ancient Greece. The first recorded Games took place in 776 BCE in the city of Olympia, in honor of the god Zeus. Initially, the competition included only a single footrace. Over time, events like wrestling, chariot racing, and the discus throw were added. Athletes came from city-states across Greece, and a truce was declared during the Games so that competitors could travel safely. The modern Olympics were revived in Athens in 1896, inspired by the ancient tradition, and have since grown to include hundreds of sports and thousands of athletes from every nation.

The modern revival was largely the work of Pierre de Coubertin, a French educator who believed that international competition could build understanding between nations. The 1896 Athens Games were modest by today''s standards: roughly 240 athletes, all of them men, representing about 14 nations. Women first competed four years later, at the Paris Games of 1900. A separate Winter Olympics was added in 1924, and for decades it was held in the same year as the Summer Games; since 1994 the two have been staggered, so that each still occurs every four years but an Olympics arrives every two. What began as a single race on a field in Greece now asks host cities to plan and build for the better part of a decade.'
