-- ── 7. From ARPANET to the Web ── 107 → 223 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'The internet we use today grew from a much smaller network created in 1969 called ARPANET, funded by the United States Department of Defense. ARPANET connected computers at a handful of universities and research labs, allowing scientists to share data across long distances for the first time. Over the following decades, the network expanded and the rules governing how computers communicate — called protocols — were standardized. In 1991, scientist Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web, a system of linked pages that made the internet far easier for ordinary people to navigate. Today, billions of people use the internet daily for communication, commerce, education, and entertainment.

That growth depended on something easy to overlook: no one owns the rules. The protocols are published openly, and in 1993 the European physics laboratory where Berners-Lee worked placed the software for the World Wide Web in the public domain, free for anyone to use. Any company could then write its own browser or run its own server, so long as it followed the shared standards. Access, however, has never been equal. The International Telecommunication Union estimates that about a third of the world''s people have still never been online, most of them in lower-income countries where service costs too much or networks do not reach. The network that connects billions has not yet connected everyone.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%The internet we use today grew from%'
