-- ── 8. How Vaccines Work ── 108 → 232 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Vaccines work by training the immune system to recognize and defeat specific pathogens before a real infection occurs. When a vaccine is administered, it introduces a harmless version of a pathogen — such as a weakened or inactivated virus, or just a piece of its protein — into the body. The immune system detects this foreign material and produces antibodies, proteins designed to neutralize that specific threat. If the person later encounters the real pathogen, the immune system "remembers" it and responds rapidly, often stopping the disease before symptoms develop. This principle, called immunological memory, is what makes vaccination one of the most powerful tools in public health.

Vaccination also protects people who are not vaccinated. When enough of a community is immune, a pathogen struggles to find new hosts and outbreaks fizzle out, which shields infants too young for their shots and patients whose illnesses or medical treatments make vaccination unsafe for them. The threshold varies by disease: measles spreads so easily that roughly 95 percent of a population must be immune to keep it from circulating. That is why coverage across a whole community matters, not only what any one family decides. The clearest evidence of what widespread vaccination can accomplish is smallpox, a disease that killed millions for centuries; after a worldwide campaign, the World Health Organization declared it eradicated in 1980, and no natural case has occurred since.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%Vaccines work by training the immune system%'
    AND passage NOT LIKE '%Vaccination also protects people who%';

-- ── 9. Bell and the Telephone ── 108 → 223 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'The invention of the telephone in 1876 transformed human communication forever. Alexander Graham Bell, a Scottish-born inventor working in Boston, is credited with creating the first practical telephone and receiving the first patent for the device. Bell''s inspiration came partly from his work with deaf students and his interest in understanding how sound travels. His first successful telephone call was made to his assistant, Thomas Watson, with the now-famous words: "Mr. Watson — come here — I want to see you." Although other inventors were working on similar devices at the same time, Bell filed his patent just hours before a competing inventor, securing his place in history.

The patent Bell received, number 174,465, has been called one of the most valuable ever granted, and it was fought over for years. Rival claimants insisted that they had reached the idea first, and Bell''s company was pulled into hundreds of lawsuits defending its rights. It won every one of them. In 1888 the Supreme Court upheld Bell''s patents, settling the question of ownership in his favor. That protection did not last forever. The patent expired in 1893, and independent companies rushed into a business Bell''s firm had held almost alone. The courtroom, as much as the workbench, decided who profited from the telephone, and it shows what a single filing date can be worth.'
