-- ── 10. How Antibiotics Fight Bacteria ── 108 → 230 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Antibiotics are medicines that fight bacterial infections by targeting features of bacteria that human cells do not have. Some antibiotics work by disrupting the construction of a bacterium''s cell wall — without a protective wall, the bacterium bursts and dies. Others interfere with the bacteria''s ability to copy their genetic material or produce essential proteins, effectively stopping reproduction. Antibiotics do not work against viruses, which is why doctors do not prescribe them for colds or the flu. Overusing antibiotics has led to a growing problem called antibiotic resistance, in which bacteria evolve to survive the drugs that once killed them — a serious threat to public health worldwide.

Resistance is not a surprise; it is evolution running on a very fast clock. Bacteria can divide every twenty minutes or so, and in a population of billions a few cells almost always carry a mutation that blunts a drug''s effect. When antibiotics are used carelessly, the vulnerable bacteria die and those few survivors inherit the field, passing the trait to their descendants. It is the bacteria that change, not the patient. Alexander Fleming, who discovered penicillin in 1928, warned of exactly this in his 1945 Nobel lecture, cautioning that doses too small to kill microbes would instead teach them to survive. Within a few years of penicillin''s wide use, hospitals were already meeting staph infections the drug could no longer touch.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%Antibiotics are medicines that fight bacterial infections%'
    AND passage NOT LIKE '%surprise; it is evolution running%';

-- ── 11. The Urban Heat Island ── 109 → 226 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Cities are often several degrees warmer than the rural areas surrounding them — a phenomenon scientists call the urban heat island effect. This happens because buildings, roads, and parking lots absorb and store heat from the sun during the day and release it slowly at night, whereas natural surfaces like grass and soil reflect more heat and cool more quickly. Dark-colored materials such as asphalt absorb especially large amounts of solar energy. Additionally, vehicles, air conditioners, and industrial equipment generate heat directly. The urban heat island effect can raise energy costs, worsen air quality, and increase the risk of heat-related illness, particularly for vulnerable populations during summer heat waves.

The effect is not spread evenly within a city. Researchers who measure temperatures block by block on the same summer afternoon find that neighborhoods shaded by mature trees stay noticeably cooler than nearby blocks where pavement and rooftops dominate. Because tree cover tends to follow old patterns of investment, the hottest blocks are often the ones whose residents have the fewest resources for coping with heat, including older adults and people with chronic illnesses. Some cities have responded by coating roofs with reflective paint, planting street trees, and adding small shaded parks. These measures do work, but they work slowly: a newly planted tree may need fifteen years of growth before its canopy shades much of anything.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%Cities are often several degrees warmer than%'
