-- ── 12. Supply, Demand, and Prices ── 114 → 231 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'The law of supply and demand is one of the most fundamental principles in economics. When a product is in high demand but supply is limited, sellers can charge higher prices because buyers are competing for a scarce resource. When supply is plentiful but demand is low, prices tend to fall as sellers compete to attract buyers. The point where the quantity sellers want to sell equals the quantity buyers want to buy is called the equilibrium price. In the real world, prices shift constantly as supply and demand change — a drought that reduces the wheat harvest, for example, raises bread prices, while a new technology that makes production cheaper can lower them.

The rule has limits, however, because buyers do not react to a price change the same way for every product. When the price of a necessity such as household electricity or a prescription medicine rises, most families keep buying nearly as much as before, since there is no easy substitute; economists call this kind of demand inelastic. For an optional purchase such as a restaurant dinner or a concert ticket, the same price increase can send sales sharply lower. Expectations matter as well. If buyers believe a price is about to climb, many will rush to buy immediately, and that rush raises demand today and pushes the price up before the shortage they feared has actually arrived.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%The law of supply and demand is%'
    AND passage NOT LIKE '%rule has limits, however, because%';

