Cones, however, need a fair amount of light to do their work. In dim conditions the eye relies mostly on rods, a second kind of cell in the retina that is far more sensitive to faint light but cannot distinguish wavelengths at all. That is why a room lit only by moonlight appears gray: the objects in it still reflect exactly the same wavelengths they did at noon, but the cells reporting to the brain no longer separate one wavelength from another. The three-cone arrangement is also not the only one possible. Many birds carry a fourth type of cone that responds to ultraviolet light, so markings on feathers and flowers that are invisible to us stand out plainly to them.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%The human eye can distinguish millions of%'
