-- ── 16. How the Brain Reads Color ── 116 → 238 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Color is not just a feature of the physical world — it is a creation of the mind. When light strikes an object, the object absorbs some wavelengths and reflects others. The reflected wavelengths reach our eyes and are interpreted by the brain as color. Because the brain also uses context to make sense of color, two identical colors can appear different depending on what surrounds them. This is why a gray square looks darker when placed on a white background than when placed on a black one. Marketers use this knowledge to influence behavior: studies show that red can increase excitement and urgency, blue is associated with trust and calm, and yellow draws attention quickly.

The brain''s reliance on context also explains why colors stay recognizable when the light changes. A sheet of paper looks white at noon and still looks white under a yellowish lamp at night, even though the light bouncing off it is very different in each case. The brain compares the paper with everything around it and corrects for the lighting, a habit so automatic that people rarely notice it. That same habit limits what a single color can do. Researchers who test color effects in a laboratory often find weaker results in real stores, where price tags, packaging, lighting, and shelf position all compete for a shopper''s attention. Color shapes a first impression, but it seldom decides a purchase on its own.'
