-- ── 17. Wayfinding Across the Pacific ── 118 → 236 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Long before the invention of the compass or GPS, indigenous navigators of the Pacific Islands crossed thousands of miles of open ocean using only natural cues. By reading the stars — particularly the position and movement of key constellations — they could determine latitude and direction. They also studied ocean swells, which move in predictable patterns from distant wind sources, and used the feel of wave motion beneath their canoes to stay on course. The color of the water, cloud formations, and even the behavior of birds indicated the presence of nearby islands. This sophisticated system of wayfinding, passed down through oral tradition and practice, enabled Polynesian peoples to settle islands across a vast region of the Pacific.

