-- ── 18. Tectonic Plates and Earthquakes ── 119 → 240 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Earth''s outer layer is not a single solid shell but a patchwork of giant rocky slabs called tectonic plates. These plates fit together like pieces of a puzzle and move slowly — just centimeters per year — driven by heat rising from Earth''s interior. Where plates meet, tremendous forces build up over time. When the stress becomes too great, the plates suddenly slip or grind past one another, releasing energy in waves that shake the ground — an earthquake. Volcanoes also tend to form near plate boundaries, since magma can force its way to the surface along cracks between plates. Most of the world''s earthquakes and volcanoes occur along the "Ring of Fire," a zone encircling the Pacific Ocean.

Knowing why earthquakes happen has not made it possible to say when one will happen. Scientists can map faults, measure how fast the ground on each side is straining, and estimate the chance of a large quake in the next few decades, but they cannot name a day. With warning so limited, most protection comes from preparing in advance. Engineers design buildings that bend and sway instead of snapping, anchor them firmly to their foundations, and put flexible joints in gas and water lines. Communities practice drills so people know to drop and take cover. Where such steps have been taken, death tolls have fallen sharply; where older buildings are common, a quake of the same size can be far deadlier.'
