-- ── 20. How Sound Travels ── 121 → 240 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Sound is a form of energy that travels as waves — but unlike light, it cannot travel through empty space. When an object vibrates, it pushes against nearby air molecules, creating areas of compression and expansion that ripple outward from the source. These pressure waves travel through the air — or through solids and liquids — until they reach the ear, where they cause the eardrum to vibrate. The brain interprets these vibrations as sound. The speed of sound depends on the medium it travels through: it moves faster through water than air, and faster through steel than water. The pitch of a sound is determined by the frequency of the waves — more waves per second produce a higher pitch.

Because sound bounces off surfaces and moves at a known speed, an echo can be used to measure distance. A ship''s sonar sends a pulse toward the seafloor and times how long the return takes; since sound travels about 1,500 meters per second in seawater, a four-second round trip means the bottom is roughly 3,000 meters down. Animals do the same with far more precision. A bat calls out in pulses too high for human ears and reads the returning echoes to find a moth in the dark, correcting its aim as the echoes change. Dolphins hunt this way in cloudy water. Nothing unusual is happening in these cases, only the ordinary behavior of pressure waves put to work.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
    AND passage LIKE '%Sound is a form of energy that%'
    AND passage NOT LIKE '%Because sound bounces off surfaces%';

-- ── 21. The Life Cycle of Stars ── 127 → 235 words
UPDATE questions SET passage = 'Stars are not permanent fixtures in the sky — they are born, live for millions or billions of years, and eventually die. A star begins as a cloud of gas and dust called a nebula. Gravity pulls this material together, and when the core grows hot enough, nuclear fusion ignites and a star is born. For most of its life, a star burns hydrogen fuel. When the hydrogen runs out, the star expands into a red giant. Small stars like our sun will eventually shed their outer layers and leave behind a dense core called a white dwarf. Massive stars, however, end dramatically: they collapse and explode in a violent supernova, sometimes leaving behind an incredibly dense remnant called a neutron star or even a black hole.

These endings matter beyond astronomy. Hydrogen and helium have existed since the early universe, but nearly every heavier element was assembled inside stars. Fusion in stellar cores builds carbon, oxygen, and iron, and a supernova hurls that material outward at enormous speed, mixing it into the thin gas and dust between the stars. The calcium in a person''s bones and the oxygen in each breath were made this way, long before Earth existed. Such explosions are rare but unforgettable: in 1054, astronomers in China recorded a sudden light in the sky, visible in daylight for more than three weeks. The debris cloud it left is still expanding today.'
  WHERE section = 'reading'
