     'Democracy — the system of government in which citizens hold political power — traces its origins to ancient Athens, Greece, in the late 6th century BCE (around 508 BCE). Athenian statesman Cleisthenes introduced reforms that gave citizens a direct voice in governing the city-state, replacing the rule of aristocrats with a system called direct democracy. In this system, eligible citizens gathered in an assembly and voted on laws and policies themselves, rather than electing representatives. However, participation was limited: women, enslaved people, and foreigners were excluded. Despite these flaws, ancient Athens established the foundational idea that ordinary people, not only kings or nobles, had the right to shape their own government.

